Zero 0.10 local prepared-release updater

The delivered programs are original Zero compiled directly to native Linux
x64 ELF and Windows x64 PE. They do not launch a shell, Python, PowerShell,
CMD, an external hash utility or a foreign language runtime. build.py and the
test scripts are external development/verification orchestration only.
The updater manages a separate local store and preserves installer manifest v1,
its base image and storage file. It does not modify a running or booted OS.

Native commands:
  zero-update init SOURCE.iso VERSION NEW_DIRECTORY
  zero-update stage SOURCE.iso VERSION DIRECTORY
  zero-update publish SOURCE.iso VERSION NEW_INBOX
  zero-update check INBOX DIRECTORY
  zero-update status DIRECTORY
  zero-update verify DIRECTORY
  zero-update rollback DIRECTORY
  zero-sha256 FILE
Windows uses zero-update.exe / zero-sha256.exe with the same arguments.

VERSION is a decimal release counter in 1..4294967295, not a parsed semantic
version string. The publisher chooses it; it is not inferred from ISO contents.
init creates a dedicated new directory and prepares its first image. stage
requires a strictly higher counter than any previously staged counter, even
after rollback. The ISO is copied to an exclusive temporary file, checked with
our streaming SHA256, reopened and independently hashed again, then atomically
published without replacing an existing path. Previous images are retained.
All selected/previous images must verify before any new selection is recorded.

publish creates a new local inbox containing system.iso and release.meta.
check validates that metadata and, if its counter is newer, automatically
prepares the inbox image and records the new PREPARED selection. A repeated
check is idempotent. A launcher can call check at startup; this program does
not register a daemon, scheduler or OS startup entry. There is no polling
loop or network fetch in this version. An absent/unreadable inbox is an error,
not a claim that a remote server has no update. A corrupted inbox is rejected.

Example, using trusted local release media:
  zero-update init zero.iso 900 NEW_STORE
  zero-update publish next-zero.iso 901 NEW_INBOX
  zero-update check NEW_INBOX NEW_STORE
  zero-update status NEW_STORE
  zero-update rollback NEW_STORE
Every successful selection explicitly says PREPARED and NOT IMPLEMENTED for
boot activation. rollback changes only the prepared selection and preserves
the highest counter. Repeated rollback toggles the two most recent selections.
Older historical ISO files remain untouched; no garbage collection is provided.

The store can be a new child directory of a Zero installer preparation.
It never changes installer/system.iso, storage.data or zero.install. Installer
v1 removal preserves unknown updater directories. No partition table, boot
sector, BCD, GRUB, firmware entry or user data disk is modified. The optical
image format checker accepts the current Zero BIOS/UEFI catalog layout and a
consistent ZERO_SYSTEM ISO9660 envelope, including in-file catalog boot-image
extents; it does not prove a new image boots.
Images are limited to 1GiB. Actual boot testing remains a separate release gate.

The supplied native file API rejects nonregular files and final symlinks or
Windows reparse points. It does not secure linked ancestor directories or
provide a sandbox against another process changing the directory. Use a
private owned directory. An exclusive .update-lock prevents cooperating updater
instances from mutating the store simultaneously. Status/verify read without
creating a lock and fail closed if concurrent/incomplete state is observed.
Existing temporary, lock, version, metadata and destination files are never
silently overwritten or deleted. Normal copy failures delete only the current
invocation's exclusive temporary payload. Unrelated contents are preserved.

Journal publication is append-only. A successful append is closed, reopened
and byte-verified before reporting success. Each fixed record has a format,
sequence, transition invariants, commit marker and FNV-1a64 corruption check;
image digests are SHA256. A torn journal tail is an error. A failed partial
append retains .update-lock and all ISO files for inspection. Automatic journal
truncation or stale-lock deletion is deliberately absent. Recovery requires
restoring a known owned journal backup and removing its stale lock after
confirming no updater is running. A crash between payload publication and the
journal can leave an unselected ISO; it is preserved and blocks reuse of that
filename. Maximum journal length is 4096 records; reaching it fails safely.
No fsync/flush primitive exists in the shared file API, so successful power-loss
durability or automatic crash recovery is not claimed.

SHA256 detects byte corruption; it does not authenticate a publisher. Neither
release.meta nor zero.updates is digitally signed. There is no configured
release server, TLS transport, trusted signing key, public URL or remote update
service. Do not treat an untrusted inbox as an authenticated update. Booting
an ISO inside a host filesystem and activating an updated boot slot are still
unimplemented. This is a real native local preparation/verification/rollback
backend, not a complete installed rolling-release operating system.

Source layout:
  declarations.zero.part     shared native variables/memory
  sha256.zero.part           original streaming SHA256/hash-and-copy worker
  main.zero.part             updater commands, journal and filesystem logic
  hash-main.zero.part        standalone SHA256 CLI
  update.zero / sha256.zero  exact combined standalone Zero source files
  FORMAT.txt                persistent data contract
  verification/*.json        actual test results and tested hashes

Build with the repository's native host API compiler binaries:
  python3 updater/build.py
This combines authored Zero fragments without translating their logic, then
runs the own compiler. The resulting binaries need no build script at runtime.
Linux verification: python3 updater/test-native.py
Windows PE verification under the existing Wine: python3 updater/test-windows.py
Wine evidence does not establish native Windows reparse or crash behavior.
